<<~ ahu #/the-attractor>>

!! The Pono Roadmap ~ the attractor reads STABLE FOUNDING

''STABLE FOUNDING'' names the state the house grows toward (operator-ruled). Once a Lararium stands, its
base state reads STABLE: a PRIVATE household SEED (its own charter, one seat) for the operator's own devices, and a FOUNDED PERSONA
ZERO. Everything else flows downstream of it — contracting into other Nexuses and founding a Nexus with a second operator, inviting the operator's own
PersonaGroup devices, and hosting guests who walk.

This carrier holds the house's ONE roadmap, and ONLY what still binds or stays owed. A cold session that
reads the boot seed and then this carrier can pick up any thread: the instruments and the law that bites
(#/cold-start), the rulings (#/rulings), the design decisions that still shape unbuilt work (#/decisions),
the six paths in one order (#/the-road), every open loop beside the train that closes it (#/the-loops),
each train's commits in order (#/the-trains), the lanes that run beside the road (#/side-lanes), the fresh
start (#/fresh-start), and what the operator still owns (#/open). Landed work reads in git and the
CHANGELOG, never here. No count rests here: each reading names the instrument that takes it. Law lives in
its own carrier, and this carrier points there.

''Full models land early'' (operator ruling; quote license: //"Full caps now … Full models in early, so we don't code churn later."//). The road takes no staging by milestone: a slice builds its whole model, with its whole capability reach, in the place the order gives it, and no slice ships a narrower interim that a later slice must re-cut.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/cold-start>>

!! Cold start ~ what a cold session reads, runs and obeys

!!! Read, in this order
# <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/noosphere-boot">>, the boot seed; then this carrier, top to bottom.
# <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/hearths">> before the first commit: which session holds which ground, and the commit isolation that guards hands inside one session.
# <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/working-procedures">> before the first test or wire: the red-first procedure in eight moves, the ladder for wiring a module that stands unreached, the instruments that read green while they lie, and the working agreements. Before any grammar work, <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/grammar-watch">>.
# Bring every ruling #/open names to the operator before a lane builds against it.

!!! Instruments ~ run each fresh before trusting a reading
* ''Branch'': `feature/lararium-node-5`. `git rev-list --count origin/feature/lararium-node-5..HEAD` reads the unpushed commits. The controlling session pushes, only a head whose shard run read green and only while no CI run stands in flight (`git push origin <sha>:refs/heads/feature/lararium-node-5`); no lane pushes. Green has two halves: the shard run, and the branch's full CI, which `node tools/ci-verdict.mjs --head <sha>` reads as green, red or unread. The same command prints a third reading beside them, the held-out container drivers: the run that names the head, or else the newest run the branch holds, with that run's head, the commits between and each driver's result. A head no held-out run measured reads unread there, never green; the third reading says and never moves the exit code. Never a lane's to move, commit, stash or revert: `.worktrees/`, `.claude/worktrees/`, the `mempalace` submodule pointer, and the `VSCode-TW5-Syntax` pointer, which the operator pins at the released grammar while the grammar's own head stands ahead of that pin.
* ''Unit suites'': each package's own, under a scratch `LAR_ROOT` with `LAR_BAGS` unset; `node --test tools/*.test.mjs` for the tools. An import failure reads as zero tests, so read the file count beside the test count. `pnpm -r test` skips the root `tests/` directory by design.
* ''Typecheck'': `tools/typecheck-witness.sh`. Typecheck reads source and the binary reads `dist`, so run the built binary after any change that crosses packages.
* ''Witness fleet'': `bash tools/witness-all.sh`, the whole fleet, one run at a time, by the controlling session and never by a lane that works beside other hands; a hand-picked subset reads greener than the tree. It holds out the witnesses its own `HELD_OUT` table names (the docker, browser and tree-clean ones). Capture its exit code directly, never through a pipe; `git status --porcelain` reads identical before and after a sound run.
* ''E2E'': every set rides the CI shards (`tools/e2e-shards.mjs`, `.github/workflows/e2e-shards.yml`; `E2E_SETS` names the sets and the merge gate checks each set's union). `node tools/e2e-shards.mjs push <lane>` pushes HEAD to a throwaway `ci/<lane>` branch. A local run of one file proves that file alone.
* ''CI'' (`.github/workflows/ci.yml`): `gh run list --branch feature/lararium-node-5` names the run for the pushed head; a later commit waits for the next push. `held-out.yml` runs on every push to a feature branch and to `main`, weekly, and by hand (`gh workflow run held-out.yml --ref <branch>`). It builds the product image once, runs one hosted job per cell (`node tools/held-out-cells.mjs cells` names them: each whole driver, and each scenario `tools/mesh-scenarios.sh --list` names), and its merge job prints one `HELD-OUT-DRIVER:` line per driver and one `HELD-OUT-VERIFY:` line. A cell that never answered reads red there by name. `node tools/held-out-steps.mjs <cell>` runs one cell's job on a desk, read off the workflow.
* ''The docker mesh'' (by hand): `crossing-witness`; `node tools/mesh-pins.mjs --up` stands the mesh pinned; `herm-mesh-witness` carries `node/alpha` across three hops; `mesh-scenarios nexus` (`tools/mesh-scenarios.sh --list` names every scenario and starts no build; `MESH_PROJECT` names the run's own compose project). The mesh publishes `MESH_PORT_*` on loopback, every node service requires `LARES_HARNESS_IMAGE`, and `tools/harness-image.sh` checks its digest. A hearth whose log carries a `[lararium] fatal:` line reads as not stood.
* ''Island doors'': `tools/capability-inventory-witness.sh` measures which vessel doors (fetch · rest · custody write · dyad) each island of the node and web assemblies reaches on the built tree, and every source call that spawns an island or attaches it a door, against the committed table `tools/capability-inventory.json`. The table measures reach, never a door's policy (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/harbor#/the-harnesses">>). A door a class reaches where the table says it did not, a new or extra seam, a seam that left or a new ask reads red by name; a deliberate change re-writes the table in the commit that makes it (`node tools/capability-inventory.mjs --write`). A browser wiki or session island reaches no door and meets the dyad refusal; the browser's daemon island reads `unmeasured`, since its spawn seam stands inside `openBrowserVessel`; a session island's view asks run through the pool core's own listener, which no row drives.
* ''The red register'': `packages/lararium-mesh/tests/unbuilt-laws.test.ts` declares each law the models imply and no code enforces; `tools/unbuilt-laws-witness.sh` reports a declared red that greened.
* ''Known environmental reds'': `lararium-tw5` `tests/syncer-back-parity.e2e.test.ts` (Chromium legs) and `tests/meme-routes.e2e.test.ts` fail where no browser or server socket stands; the browser suite reads `no tests` under a `HOME` that holds no Playwright Chromium.
* ''The operator's live `~/.lares` vessel'' holds an earlier shape that this build refuses. Never stand it here; it takes the fresh start, by hand (#/fresh-start).

!!! Steps
# `export LAR_ROOT=/tmp/lar-scratch-<you>` before any in-tree boot or any `lares` command that touches the store. `LAR_ROOT` moves the store, the bags root and every projection mirror root; `LAR_BAGS` alone moves only the bags root, and the projection still writes the tracked `bags/` and `wikis/`. A staged e2e vessel takes its own scratch `LAR_ROOT` and no global `LAR_BAGS`. One `lares meme normalize|check` reads files and needs only a scratch `LAR_BAGS`.
# `LAR_ROOT` leaves these homes where they stand: `~/.mempalace` (the destructive `mempalace repave` among its readers), `~/.venv`, `~/.local/bin`, and the harness homes `~/.claude`, `~/.codex`, `~/.copilot`. A `vessel stand --codex` under a scratch root still writes the operator's real harness home.
# Build: `pnpm install --frozen-lockfile` only where a lead approved an install, then `pnpm -r build`, then `pnpm --filter @lararium/tw5 build:tw5-vendor` (the TW5 core blob that `head-parity` and the plugin smoke need), then `node tools/stamp-build.mjs`. A fresh `git worktree` holds empty submodules; populate them before building there.
# ''A founding wants a human route on a TTY.'' With no TTY it refuses `CredentialTtyRefusal`, and no flag or environment variable carries a credential. A harness founds through the test door: `tools/test-scratch-marker.mjs` stages the root, `tools/seed-test-custody.mjs` names the published test route, then `lares vessel found --skip-build`; `tools/unlock-test-custody.mjs` opens a marked scratch root. An agent may read vessel state and hand the operator the next founding command as text; the operator performs each founding and custody act.
# ''Every boot rests at the floor.'' A verb that needs sovereign state refuses at a standing floor and names `lares vault unlock`. A harness that reads the first `gate key:` line of a log reads the carriage relay's key where a relay stands.
# ''The container'': `tools/lares-container.sh` offers `up`, `down`, `found` and `logs` (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/harbor">>). ''The browser surface'': `tools/browser-ci.sh` over `docker-compose.browser-ci.yml`, one project and image tag per builder (`BROWSER_CI_PROJECT`, `BROWSER_CI_IMAGE`).
# The tw5 plugin bundle (`packages/lararium-tw5/plugins/lares-memetic-wikitext.json`, `plugins/standalone/*`, `src/plugin-tiddler.generated.ts`) rides in git and must equal a fresh build: rebuild after any tw5 source or tiddler change and commit it with the change. CI's `build-freshness` job checks it. `genesis/` carries that bundle, the boot shadows and the engine blob by address, and the wiki inside a founded vessel runs the bytes genesis carries; a bake (`pnpm --filter @lararium/node build:genesis`) lands as its own labelled `GENESIS RE-BAKE` commit with the CAS change, and a vessel founded on the earlier seed re-founds. `tools/genesis-parity-witness.sh` reads the gap: a bundle that runs ahead of genesis reads red until a bake closes it or a row in `genesis/unbaked.json` declares it.
# `lares-cli` `tests/pour-repave.test.ts` needs the dev-root marker (`LAR_DEV_REPO_ROOT=1` or the ignored `lar-dev-root.json`).
# Crew shape, by standing grant: one Opus lead drives two Luna builders and one Sol reviewer, five rounds per set at most, and the solo chain runs beside as many side lanes as hold disjoint files. A builder returns a patch on a narrow one-module brief; the lead applies, builds, tests and commits in its own worktree, then lands whole commits with `git commit -F <msg> --only -- <paths>` after rebuilding the touched `dist`, since the commit hook runs the built CLI. A lead copies its resume sheet out of the session scratch directory after each commit: a restart empties that directory.

!!! The standing law that bites ~ read the carrier before arguing with it
* ''The frame stands as canon'': DOCTYPE, the SOH bearing, STX, `toml meta` first in the body, the body, ETX with its `ni:///sha-256;…` check, EOT. One span per carrier; a spelling outside the frame grammar tears the frame (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/memetic-wikitext-framing">>).
* ''The frame's `?` and the gate's unechoed key stay distinct.'' `from="?"` declares an unresolved bearing at the grammar layer; the gate withholds a known key at the transport layer. Their one shared law reads at <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/system-pattern-integrities#/threshold-pattern-integrities">>.
* ''Ingest decides from a parse and a verdict.'' The deserializer stands as the pure meme-to-tiddlers function; `verdict()` (Match · Stale · Absent · Torn · Bare) lives in `@lararium/memetic-frame`; checks live in `carrier-check.ts`, repairs in normalize, policy at each door. A stale check on a human edit warns; a torn frame refuses; text with no frame reads as bare data, never a meme.
* ''Never refuse on the stock syncer path''; surface on the alert rail (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/syncer-back-parity">>).
* ''The quoteblock floor fences at two grains'': the whole chunk inside the deserializer, and a child's body in place at the child gate (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/ahu#/quoteblock-floor">>).
* ''An admit carries across a seal roll only through its roll anchors.'' The gate reads the presented admit against the carriage board's counted revokes and the kapae antigen, and folds no allow roster (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/membership-doctrine">>).
* ''No global now'' and ''the fence'': #/rulings.
* ''Grounds before wins'': rank work by what inherits shape from what, never by confidence in the next wire (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/working-procedures#/grounds-before-wins">>).
* ''One holder per store, one Lararium per human'': a second holder meets `StoreHeld` across containers and namespaces (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/harbor">>).
* ''Fragments'': every `lar:` fragment roots at `#/…`, one raw `#`, `/` nests, `$` reserved (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/memetic-wikitext">>).
* ''Grammar lives in the tiddlers'' (`packages/lararium-tw5/tiddlers/sigil-*.tid` generate `grammar-table.generated.ts`); sigil heads stay core ASCII; `aka` pins as information, `kanawai` pins as binding, `kahea` transcludes live.
* ''Early alpha'': no back-compat, no shims, no legacy recovery; a stale thing aligns to the current frame and grammar, and the fence (#/rulings) ends that line (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/wild-bytes">>). Derive, never enumerate. One function, one relation.
* ''A registry compiled about subjects refuses at any scope'' (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/registry-filter">>).
* ''A relation's evidence keeps its type'' and ''a worker keeps its own storage scope'': <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/causal-island#/selected-contracts">>.
* ''Shared tree'': never `git stash`; commit only your paths; never `--no-verify`; a spirit spawns no helper and never juggles another hand's uncommitted hunks; read an exit code directly; prove a zero with a known positive (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/hearths#/law">>).
* ''The node side routes nothing into the shore's files'' while a shore lane holds them: `packages/lararium-browser/**`, `packages/lararium-web/**`, `tw5-host-bridge.ts`, `tw5-projection.ts`.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/rulings>>

!! Rulings ~ the operator's, as approved

!!! The live test and the fence
* ''The live test runs with TWO operators on two machines from its first day'': the operator and the operator's daughter, in one house. The face-switch act, the cabal plane, the custody seal and the engine fold ALL land before it.
* ''The fence.'' The second operator's SEAT beside the first at the first Nexus's founding ENDS the alpha line. Every destroy-class or shape-changing act lands BEFORE it: the engine re-mint and the genesis re-bake; the carrier and key-class changes; the Stable re-found (AID, charter, seat); every signed-shape change; every load-bearing rename. After it, data deserves a migration path.
* ''A working Lararium comes first'': Live Wiki Browser -><- Live Node, under manual QA on one machine. Sensorium work waits behind that milestone.
* ''A founding road carries its own succession.'' A reconstitution kit lets another competent operator stand a node and read its grammar; a rite held in one operator's head alone carries no succession.
* ''Face plurality stands as a day-one requirement.'' Each of the two humans wears at least three faces, each with a public Handle, on the first Nexus.

!!! The house as a container
* ''All CODE runs dockerized''; the host keeps only the hooks into the operator's environment (files and bags, session capture, the editor and CLI entry, custody).
* ''ONE Lararium per HUMAN, never per face.'' A human's faces keep their data together in one vessel (`PRIVATE-all, PUBLIC-one`); the recorded face-switch act carries plurality. No design assumes a container or a state mount per face.
* ''A SESSION wears the face; the vessel wears none'' (operator-ruled; quote license: //"a human might want a tab open as one Persona, and a tab open as another Persona... same vessel. Identity as relation."// and //"a CLI session wears its own face too … those act as ephemeral UX"//). A vessel holds its roots and one store. A session — a browser tab, a CLI run — contracts in as ONE persona, and several sessions may live at once over one vessel, each wearing its own. Every act carries exactly one face. Two faces live from one vessel never link on the wire: each session proves itself under its own face-scoped key, never under a key the vessel shares across them. The vessel-wide parts (custody, the daemon island) serve every session and let none read another's planes.
* ''Local-first first.'' Cloud "backup larariums" under other humans' delegated keys stand DEFERRED until the operator can deploy to a cloud surface. Nothing in the container or the face-switch work may foreclose a keyless ciphertext carrier later.
* ''The boundary'' (settled by the house under those rulings): one multi-arch image; `/workspace` mounts bags and wikis, `/state` mounts vessel homes and custody, never an image layer; the local socket stays INSIDE the container and the host CLI enters through `docker compose exec`; the app never receives the docker socket; mDNS offers unauthoritative LAN candidates and a pinned admission alone grants authority; DNS-01 wildcard TLS supplies browser secure contexts; a release manifest binds the image digest to the genesis, engine and grammar CIDs.

!!! No global now
* ''A wall clock never decides whether an act runs.'' Abandonment, revocation and expiry ride causal events; a timer serves only its holder's own liveness, and a device clock may sit under an adversary's hand. Web2.5 timers (TLS acceptance, OAuth tokens) sit at the edge of the causal island.
* ''Lapse rides a causal fence'': an epoch roll, a burn marker, or causal supersession at a verified relation fence. A replica missing that history reports an incomplete view, never "expired"; quiet never means lapse.

!!! The Stable state and its consequences
* ''Every Lararium holds a charter.'' One operator's charter reads as a SEED and two operators' seats in one charter read as a NEXUS (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/kahu#/seed-and-nexus">>). A household seed never grows into a Nexus; a Nexus founds fresh at threshold two of two. One "I" may hold several PersonaGroups. A Nexus grows by adding seats through its own rolls.
* ''A household seed serves its holder alone.'' A one-operator seed gives durable use without recruiting a mesh; useful local standing precedes every wider relation.
* ''Guests walk the household seed.'' Planes stay persona-scoped, so a guest sees its own documents plus the public and infrastructure shelf.
* ''Add-a-face (A).'' The daemon writes the public half; the terminal seals the recovery share; seating stays a separate act.
* ''Every leaf starts at the floor with its own root.'' A human may later JOIN a leaf to a PersonaGroup so its devices sync as one fleet; joining never takes the device's own root away.
* ''Founding persona zero belongs to standing'', never to a later verb. A capable vessel founds its household seed, so a "vessel with no charter" reads Mu.
* ''Stable folds into the re-found'' as its core.
* ''Forks report per board'': every presenter names every fork on the board it reads (the KERI watcher shape).

!!! Planes ~ cabal, realm, Nexus
* ''CABAL names the collective, REALM holds shared resources, NEXUS names the federated hardware mesh.'' The Nexus `@crossroads` board serves public-infrastructure docs and the Kahu Cabal keeps them. An operator's offering to a book club belongs in that club's realm, signed by the face worn there.
* ''Kahu drafts in the realm the charter derives''; publishing to `@crossroads` stands as a separate, recorded promotion act. A second Kahu realm gets made only for a genuinely different relation.
* ''Promotion approves the EXACT BYTES'' (hash, purpose, surface) under a threshold the charter sets, with a higher bar for charter, admission and trust-root changes. A CID, a clerk's edit or an active persona never stands in for collective approval.
* ''Steward signing.'' A scoped face signs acts that belong to one person; a transparent quorum Handle signs acts the collective owns; the root never appears publicly. A human wears MANY faces and many public Handles, each in many cabals, and signs a steward edge with the face or Handle worn IN THAT CABAL.
* ''Oracle answer'': one public answer per Nexus, signed by that Nexus's Kahu steward; a herm carries and presents it and signs nothing public. The engine floor rides as one shared content-addressed bake, and each answer names its floor CID (operator ruling).
* ''Herm tending'': tending may reach the herm's key; the house accepts that reach and names it (operator ruling).
* ''Introduction and admission'': a user introduces; an operator admits; the answer consumes the introduction (operator ruling).
* ''Names and counters'': a counter orders one writer's own emissions only, and the face lives at the session capability. The per-Nexus convergence secret and its keyring leave the model: nothing derives from one, the Stable founding act yields none, no seat act commits to one and no admission delivers one. (operator ruling; <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/cap-tier#/key-delivery">>).
* ''Kahu acts fall into four chartered classes'': ordinary (1-of-1); high-impact — charter, admission, trust root (3-of-5, or 2-of-3 for a small body); emergency (2-of-3, a narrow pre-authorized power reviewed by a later causal act); revocation (the threshold of the original act). A challenge appends to the record and may pause LOCAL reliance; it never halts the protocol. Admission of a Kahu or a Lamplighter always takes the seat quorum, and no ordinary single-seat class covers it; kapae and rolls take the quorum too; the user road rides its own bound (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/kahu#/seed-and-nexus">>).
* ''Renewal runs as three sequences, kept apart'' (the realm feed, a per-renewal causal position, the KEL), and lapse arrives as three distinct events: signed departure, recorded removal by relation-scoped kapae, renewal supersession.
* ''A cancel crossing a herm yields three receipts'': the caller's abandonment, the relay's delivery (transit only), the vessel's outcome. A caller timeout never counts as a cancel; eviction answers `retention-gap`.
* ''One face at a time names WHO ANSWERS for an act.'' The switch law reads in `lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/persona-policy#/one-face`.
* ''A session wears the face; the vessel wears none'' (operator ruling).

!!! Custody ~ the Vessel KEK
* One Vessel KEK (VK), minted at the Stable founding, lives only inside LUKS-style keyslots, each a Clevis `sss` tree. Pin kinds: passphrase · paper (SLIP-39) · ember · tpm (one share only).
* ''EMBER'' names the Tang-shaped, per-relation device pin; it holds no share of VK. Only the blinded points cross on unlock.
* ''A backup route stands at every tier.'' Every keyslot tree, household or civic, keeps a human route (passphrase or paper); bind refuses a tree without one. Embers never stand as the only way in.
* ''The floor key keeps the vessel's public name''; the sealed custody root derives the veil and the sovereign caps. The veil change stays destroy-class.
* ''Unbind offers a VK rotation loudly'', every time, never folded in silently.
* ''Locality law'': an unattended ember answers on the local link only, and only where the hearth holds a hardware freshness anchor the ember checks on each ask (#/rulings, the ember's key at rest); a relay pin wants an attending hand. The vessel rests at the floor and raises when t embers answer; the raise lapses by non-renewal, which reads as the raise's next causal boundary (#/rulings, how a raise lapses).
* ''HOT ⊥ COLD'': VK and keyhive state stay hot; the root, the recovery shares and the next-root seed unwrap per act.
* Measuring hardware x-only ECDH (phone enclaves) before trusting an enclave-held ember: approved, deferred.

!!! The hosted walk (F1)
* ''(c′) adopted.'' The HOST's gate reads and burns invites. The gate sorts every socket into same-operator · contracted · walker · stranger; under PRIVATE a stranger meets silence before any session frame. The guest carries its hosting grant; the hearth stores nothing about it. Invites mint by blind POPRF evaluation, so who-invited-whom stays non-computable. Revocation runs by kapae of the guest's leaf, by non-renewal, or by an epoch roll. Sybils meet tolerance, never detection.
* ''The inviter's face never signs an invite'': that signature would link the inviter at redemption. The hearth's blind evaluation carries the proof instead.
* ''V — vesting by class.'' A guest the HOST invites holds floor 1 at once; a guest a WALKER invites vests at the host's next roll. The roll reads as the household's consent to grow, and purpose labels separate the two classes without naming the minter. The built allowance carries the pre-answered dial of the user road's three; the operator's answer to a user's introduction stands owed, and until it lands a walker's token earns standing with no answer (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/membership-doctrine#/the-invite">>).
* ''W — the hearth carries a walker's own documents'' as sealed ciphertext (carry ⊥ read), under a quota per guest. The PersonaGroup holds the durable copy; hearth carriage stays a convenience, never a backup.
* ''W reclaim.'' Quota pressure alone triggers it; lapse reads as a RATIO against the guest's own rhythm between contacts, so the host's roll cadence cancels; the most-lapsed-for-itself goes first; notice on the guest's next contact comes before any discard. The ruling sets aside counting the host's rolls: it measures the host's diligence, and diligence confers no title.
* ''N — a node may walk.'' The sorter sorts by proven relation, never by device type.

!!! The cleanup of the hosted walk (B1)
* ''The worn face hosts and walks.'' No roster order picks anything; a vessel wearing no face hosts and walks nowhere. Before its first hosting act in a Nexus, a roll refuses when the hosting doc holds an act this face signed that no local state holds.
* ''A herm answers the knock.'' A charterless herm answers any proven peer that knocked (`ownPlacePosture`): a waystone by class, never a broken lararium. A lararium reads its charter, and an absent or torn charter reads PRIVATE.
* ''A boot follows `LAR_ROOT`.'' A vessel isolated under `LAR_ROOT` projects into that root, never into the tracked tree; the mirror roots never read `LAR_BAGS`, so the isolation instruction names `LAR_ROOT`.
* ''Compartments outrank admission control.'' A walker reads its own hearth's hosting doc, never the carriage board: the carrier set stays a contract ledger read by those who hold contracts.

!!! The first ceremony, and the acts around it (operator ruling)
* ''Guests'': the first ceremony runs with the host's own invites only. The hosted road builds whole before the ceremony (#/the-attractor): its doors stand built and stay shut on the day.
* ''Reach on the day'': LAN only. The public domain's Handle table comes later.
* ''At two seats no seat signs any Kahu act alone.''
* ''An operator's own herm'': an operator contracts their own herm with one signature, scoped to one place and one Nexus, and it falls with a kapae of the operator's admit.
* ''The hosting key'': the operator's seat signs ONE delegation to a hosting key; that key signs each hosting act and seeds each epoch. A session wearing any other face signs nothing for the hearth.
* ''The cold-key card'': the house format — 64 hex plus a checksum, in groups of four — printing its purpose, chair label, generation and a short charter fingerprint.
* ''The DNS credential'': the registrar's credential rests as two files on the first operator's always-on node, outside both mounts, read by the one-shot issuer. That node issues one wildcard per machine, and the second machine receives its certificate by hand.
* ''A browser leaf holds a passphrase or paper slot before it holds a persona root''; the mint refuses without one.
* ''No guardians on the day'': each face starts one-of-one on its own paper, and enrolment comes later by a rotation on the face's own log.
* ''Shore saves, as built'': a rename onto a standing title proceeds as an ordinary write at field grain; a tombstone moved into a bag that lacks the title refuses unless the hand asks to shadow.
* ''A seat gets ADDED, never replaced.'' The long-span act adds a holder to BOTH cabals — a Kahu seat and a Lamplighter contract — and the founders keep their chairs; one founding Kahu may hold seats across many Nexuses. The road proves the roll that adds a seat, and the new holder's carriage contract, first. A passed chair stays a possible act. A Kahu who works far from the other seats needs a co-signature for every quorum act, so the partial-and-cosign door carries a partial act across a relay and back with no clock.
* ''Per-Nexus identifiers'': an admit names a per-Nexus identifier, a random leaf no other Nexus can link. The face's lineage may re-issue it by a signed hand-over the gate verifies, and per-Nexus keys mint at random, never from the persona seed.
* ''Every boot rests at the floor until a human unlocks'' (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/waking-floor">>). At the floor every fleet lane stays shut, and the custody write door, the rest door and the dyad door answer the daemon island alone. A bounded ember stays design input: it raises to SERVING alone, never signs or admits, runs on a count and never a clock, needs the device's own unlock plus a second signal, records each raise, and any other device of the human removes it.

!!! The models, each in its own carrier (operator rulings, approved as presented)
* ''Full models land early'': #/the-attractor.
* ''One roadmap'': this carrier. Standing procedures rest at <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/working-procedures">>, and the grammar watch at <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/grammar-watch">>.
* ''The sealed store'': cleartext lives in the live wiki and the operator's projected files alone. Private bytes take randomized authenticated sealing addressed over the ciphertext; the private tier takes no convergent sealing, and no opener returns unauthenticated bytes. A content key carries a version and rolls at a causal removal act. History at admission must have stood declared when written, and one logical bag name carries its key-roll epochs (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/sealed-store">>).
* ''The backup bundle'': a bundle holds ONE face, sealed under a route the person holds, and carries that face's root with its own lines and no key of any seat. Its memory rides as content keys, sealed inside the key island: a bundle opens every surviving value ever sealed under a version it carries, values written after its cut included, and no version minted later. A restore runs as a recovery act that takes a fresh veil and a new seat, and one face on a new device reads as the same audience for the versions that face already held (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/custody">>).
* ''Removal's reach'': four edges take a marker (dyad, vouch, member, carriage). A vouch edge answers to its voucher and to a chartered quorum; a seal roll carries every shadow; a standing shadow reaches every gate and drives a key roll; a contested tie keeps the shadow up; mutual removal ends in an explicit fork (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/kapae#/four-edges">>).
* ''The PersonaGroup stands as a Keyhive group'', so a seat's revocation rolls the key of every bag the group reaches (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/kapae#/keys">>; a fresh group stays owed, `K12` of #/the-loops).
* ''The hands'': every hand opens a session. A tasked spirit comes down to an AI, a Mask and the vessel's seat, attended or standing; the vessel's own key serves as a spirit's system identity; a Mask counts as no pet-name. The door carries and the hand authorizes, over one verb table, and a standing seat never signs an act that changes who holds authority (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/session-contract#/the-hands">>).
* ''Sealed-row acts ride the custody door'', never a store verb whose output lands in the daemon's document in the clear (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/waking-floor#/the-shape">>).
* ''Gate keys come in three tiers'': a floor route's keys derive from the floor key, and a sovereign route's from the sealed custody root (operator ruling); the serving route's bundle derives from SK, a third tier that leaves those two untouched (#/rulings, the ember's serving gate). A faceless place takes a fourth arm at the gate, the place arm, and a session opens before its certificate. The floor key rests outside the vessel key, and the face proof names what a holder of the disk without the passphrase reaches and never reaches (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/face-proof#/the-gate-exchange">>).
* ''The widget render boundary stands withdrawn'', and the parser carries recovery (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/api/graceful-parsing">>).

!!! House rulings under the operator's delegation ~ the questions the operator delegated, each with what flips it
The operator delegated these to the house: each stands as a house ruling the operator may overturn, its law lives in the carrier it names, and its build rides the loop rows of #/the-loops.
* ''The ember's key at rest'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-14). A separate serving key (SK), the fifth custody class `serving`, wrapped by the vessel key and by ember slots, seals the serving rows alone, and the serving tier holds no minting secret: a walker's grant checks under a public half, a shape the hosted road's re-cut lands before the ember's walker arm. An unattended answer (any slot that answers without a person's confirm on the ember) needs a freshness anchor a disk copy cannot carry, a hardware-sealed hearth key whose attestation the ember checks on each ask; without one every ember slot answers only on a person's confirm, threshold two buys a second confirm and never an unattended raise, and after any counted removal a bare deny-only `sk-rotation-owed` mark refuses every ask until SK rolls, a mark a rolled-back disk copy can omit. ''For the operator'': a hearth that holds no hardware anchor raises only on a person's confirm. The reason: a key that opens serving state opens nothing beside it, and a copied disk replays any freshness it carries; the ruling flips on a threat review showing the serving rows hold nothing a thief can use even before the grant re-cut (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/custody#/the-table">>; <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/ember">>).
* ''The ember's serving gate'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-14). A third gate tier, the serving route, takes its bundle from SK, and the floor and sovereign tiers stand untouched; a pin moves only by a signed causal event or a verified key-history step, and no quiet period ages a pin out. The reason: a dialer that pinned the sovereign tier never mistakes a serving hearth for an unlocked one. The ruling flips on proof that a gate pin decides no authority anywhere (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/face-proof#/the-gate-exchange">>).
* ''How a raise lapses'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-14). A raise lives one boot and lapses at the next causal boundary (a lock, a restart, a human unlock, a counted removal), and that boundary IS the expiry the waking floor requires; no timer and no heartbeat ends a standing, and the count spends at the raise ledger's durable commit before SERVING composes, so a crash before the commit spends nothing and serves nothing and a crash after it burns the raise. The reason: quiet never tells a slow device from a gone one; the residue: a rolled-back ledger on a disk copy restores spent raises. The ruling flips on a causal renewal event every reader receives that also survives the ember's loss (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/waking-floor#/the-shape">>).
* ''A removal no reader can keep'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-14). The based removal refuses by name where no parallel-drafts reader keeps the concurrent words, or every reader failed, and the store authors nothing; the refusal carries a durable conflict handle (the document, the removal's request id, the conflicting change and head identities, never the hand's text), so a hand whose editor went offline finds the same conflict on its return. The reason: a draft the store wrote would choose a bag, a tier and a steward no hand named. The ruling flips on an already-authorized destination for such words that keeps provenance and tier without the store choosing either (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/automerge-doc-store">>).
* ''The outbox at rest'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-14). Each outbox parcel seals under randomized authenticated encryption with a stable outbox key the vessel key wraps and an unlock hands the daemon island, the way content keys reach it, and rests under a random name with its routing inside the sealed body, so a locked disk names no reader; the `sealed-outbox` row leaves `floor-plain` for a sealed class. Before a parcel's atomic commit nothing stands sendable, after it the parcel re-opens and retries at the next unlock, and after a ship and before the local delete the receiver folds a repeat idempotently. The reason: a reader digest on a stolen disk names a relationship; no flip stands expected, since the ruling closes a residue (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/custody#/the-table">>).
* ''The herm relay leg'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-14). The ask rides the sealed sibling session under that session's seal with a tagged inner frame, and the delivery receipt stays a bare session note good for the asker alone, bound to a unique request id and the session's causal position, attesting transit and never execution; reach narrows to one PersonaGroup, and the leg lands with `W9`. The reason: the fewest new shapes, and no signing domain minted for a transport fact. The ruling flips on a concrete need for a third party to audit relay delivery after the asker vanishes (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/receipt-ledger#/the-relay-leg">>).
* ''The disk watcher's reach'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-14). A pending removal settles with no person present only under a standing instruction the operator signs by a human route, naming one bag and a bounded class of removals (a path pattern and a per-wave count ceiling): the watcher executes within it, every settled removal lands in the audit with the instruction it rode, and `lares shore restore` still answers it. An automated seat never signs one, a standoff (disk and records differ) never settles without a person, and a mass vanish past the instruction's ceiling stays pending for a person. The reason: a person who answered in writing for exactly that class has answered, and a count alone proves no intent; the ruling flips on a single incident where an in-scope instruction deleted what a person meant to keep (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/api/disk-projection#/projection-law">>).
* ''The restore's key history'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-14). A restored face stands PROVISIONAL until at least one party to its relation (a sibling device of the same human, a seated chair of its Nexus, or a guardian) shows its own log view and that view agrees with the restore's head; while provisional the face reads, recovers and serves its own content and signs no authority-changing act, the restore prints which head it read and from where, and the provisional state names who could confirm it. Where every such party stays unreachable, the guardians' recovery path (the impersonation quorum) answers, never the local read alone. The reason: no vessel reads a head it never received, and the house keeps no registry of heads; no flip stands, and the residue stays stated: one agreeing party may itself sit stale (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/custody#/the-restore">>).
* ''The Offering Antigen scope'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-14). A signed Offering act carries a stable scope, the joint Nexus AID its signed inception mints, beside `sealEpochCid` as the authorizing epoch. The receiver resolves its own charter scope, verifies the act's epoch inside that scope's locally held lineage, checks the quorum at that exact epoch, and only then folds; an act whose scope differs stays as foreign evidence outside the `kapae` and `un_kapae` fold, and an incomplete lineage reads `unavailable`. The joint inception image commits to the offer fingerprint and to both founders' fresh cold successor commitments, and a successor commitment a vault already used refuses, so the AID names one founding statement and two foundings by one roster never alias. The reason: a neighbour's condemnation copied onto another board reads as testimony, never as that board's verdict. The ruling flips on a case where two foundings by one roster must read as one Nexus (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/research/offering-antigen-scope-decision-record">>).
* ''The fork-resolve act'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-14). A fork stays a fork, and every reader names the branch it follows; no clock, no longest chain and no third-party quorum picks a branch. Two acts resolve one. ABANDON: a seal record a branch's own quorum signs at its head, closing that branch for every reader who holds it, erasing nothing and touching no sibling. REUNION: one canonical image naming the scope AID, both held heads sorted, and the new seats, threshold and successor commitments, signed by each branch's quorum at its head over identical bytes; the verifier proves both heads inside the same AID's lineages from a common ancestor, and the record becomes the common head. A seal record's predecessors form a sorted set of one or two, built before the fence; until the reunion verifier lands, a two-predecessor record refuses `REUNION_UNBUILT`. The reason: a split household reunites only by a paper both halves sign. The ruling flips on a fork that neither branch's quorum can still sign for.
* ''The re-seal proof'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-14). When a content key rolls at a counted removal, the face session that writes each replacement signs one causal re-seal record per replacement: the bag locator, the old and new key-version ids, the old and new ciphertext addresses, the logical entry it replaces and the removal act that authorized the roll. The removal quorum signs the roll, never each body. A reader with partial history verifies the signature and every binding it holds, and reports a missing parent as unverified, never as complete. The format lands with sealed-store step three, before the fence. The reason: a phone offline through the roll needs to know which photo replaced which, and whose hand re-sealed it. The ruling flips on proof that the key-version record, manifests and checkpoints already show every replacement and omission to both kinds of reader (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/sealed-store">>).
* ''Guardian enrolment's shape'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-14). Each face founds one-of-one with no guardians, and its log's verifier knows a typed first-enrolment event from the start: the face's own current key signs it, it commits the guardian set and threshold, and the face's prefix stays as it stands; every later change to the set takes the guardians' authority. The event shape and its verifier land before the fence, so an enrolment never re-founds a face. The reason: a face founded alone keeps its Handle's history when guardians join a year later, and no third hand holds standing over a one-of-one face. The ruling flips on an existing event kind that already permits that first, face-key-signed transition. Guardian count, request authentication, the veto, death and succession stay open (#/open).
* ''Engine cadence'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-14). Cadence keeps its own lineage and threshold, apart from every seat key. The founding act commits one cadence inception that names each founder's hearth, the device that bears the reboot, as a holder. Each hearth mints its cadence key at random on the device, never derived from serving or floor material, and keeps it, with its next-key commitment, as a `cold` row under the vessel key that unwraps per act; no sixth custody class and no extra card at the founding. A holder may later rotate onto a cold card by its own choice. Each island pins one engine epoch, and one daemon hosts two epochs only as two separately pinned islands. The reason: the people who bear a reboot decide it, and a seat-holder who runs no hardware forces no one's restart. The ruling flips on a cadence act that must run unattended where a per-act unwrap cannot (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/kahu#/contract">>).
* ''A realm's quorum and its boards'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-14). Each realm stands under ONE Nexus, which the vessel's first act there names and its edge ledger keeps; every reader takes the realm's quorum roster off that Nexus's charter, never off its own primary one; and the realm's boards key on the realm's own id, so a removal's record stays among its parties. The reason: authority comes from the governing charter while the record addresses the relation it affects. The ruling flips on a realm-native charter model with its own seat creation, migration and conflict rule (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/kapae#/edge-hands">>).

!!! The fresh start
* ''No pave or wipe command'' — such a command carries too much risk. See #/fresh-start.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/decisions>>

!! Decisions ~ what still shapes unbuilt work, and why

The hosted walk's own mechanics (the token, the grant, the spent-set, the hosting doc) read in
`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/membership-doctrine#/the-invite`. The decisions below constrain a slice
still on the road.

* ''One sorter, inside the gate, before the verdict.'' Every gate instance (`/ws`, `/oracle`, the membership relay) sorts before `lar:auth-ok`, so posture reads once, at admission, and never again at share time. Two readers of one posture drift. R-WIRE keeps it: one gate per (Nexus, route).
* ''The knock.'' A gate answers an upgrade only on a path derived from its own key (`gate-knock`), so a dialer holding no pin draws no HTTP 101 — the WireGuard mac1 shape. The path still rides the upgrade in the clear on plain `ws`; full client-proves-first waits for Noise IK (R-WIRE).
* ''Silence, one deadline.'' No denial frame and no close codes: one drop deadline drawn at accept, so verify time leaks nothing and a PRIVATE stranger looks exactly like a failed proof.
* ''The multi-Nexus fold.'' A gate answers strangers if ANY Nexus it stands in reads OPEN. One gate key serves every Nexus a vessel carries, so this rule reduces exactly to per-Nexus posture once each Nexus carries its own wire key (R-WIRE) — not an interim.
* ''Carriage keys on the guest's PROVEN leaf.'' A hearth-issued stub would read as a bearer capability to another guest's record and as a hearth-minted cross-lineage linker. The leaf already proves on every socket, so keying on it adds no linkage the hearth lacks; a fresh leaf opens a fresh carriage (#/open, carriage across leaves).
* ''One store door.'' A command that touches the store runs inside the standing vessel over the owner-only socket, else owns the store itself for that moment. A second Repo beside a live holder writes bytes the replica never sees. `tools/direct-repo-witness.sh` names the two lawful `new Repo(` sites and reads any other as DRIFT.
* ''No hearth-local deny set.'' Kapae and the roll suffice; a deny set would make a seizable list.
* ''No node carriage.'' A node serves as its own durable store and no story walks its carriage; ruling N holds as a sorter property and W as the hearth's service. Node carry would open a new user story, never owed by N or W.
* ''Hosting stays on the worn persona leaf.'' ROOT-ON-FOUNDER keeps a persona root on one vessel, so one face cannot host from two stores unseen; the first-roll rail surfaces the remaining case. The face-switch act inherits it.
* ''A cliff takes a funnel, then one assembled commit.'' Where one shape change crosses three packages, the fixtures funnel through one helper first; narrow builders each write one patch against a fixed contract; one lander proves them together in a scratch worktree and commits once, so the main tree never holds half a flip. Three cliffs stand on the road: the charter flip, the Nexus rename and the socket cutover.
* ''The socket's handshake changes once.'' R-WIRE and the face proof build as ONE wire train: the signed admit, then the fleet-join door, then one socket cutover (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/face-proof">>). The tree between those steps reads no earlier shape; it stands as a build order, never a compatibility mode.
* ''A pure library names its caller.'' A module built ahead of its consumer stays out of the package barrel until the commit that consumes it. Until a production path calls it, a module stands specified in code, never built.
* ''`vessel found` keeps a charterless road.'' The Stable founding act becomes the HEARTH's rite; a herm still founds as a place with no face, and a joiner still founds through an admit.
* ''The carriage verifier holds the root'': it checks the epoch, the signature AND a subject root it holds locally, and refuses by name without that root.
* ''Guardian placement'' reads as advice the house names at enrolment, never a refusal. ''QR pairing'' shows a check string on both screens after the channel opens.
* ''The floor vessel key drops its next-key commitment'', inside the one identity train. It changes the vessel's signed inception bytes; the operator may overturn it.
* ''A long-silent fleet device'' catches up before it acts: a presentation carries its presenter's frontier of the face's withdrawal history, and a gate that holds a later one answers `unconfirmed` and hands over what the device lacks. No clock; a house reading the operator may overturn (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/face-proof#/the-withdrawal-proof">>).
* ''The strangers' dial drops the NEWEST arrival'' when its slots fill; a slot frees by the operator's answer or a roll.
* ''Every browser leaf counts as evictable'': `storage.persist()` refuses in a plain tab, so no leaf holds the only copy of anything, and no passkey slot stands as the only way in (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/device-capabilities-2026">>).
* ''A restarted always-on node serves no hosted phone until a human unlocks.'' The resolver and the terminator keep answering, so the page loads and the sync waits; the page says so in one line, until the ember lands.
* ''A crossing gets recorded, never interior life.'' Only an authority, vessel, federation or externally meaningful crossing becomes distributed canon (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/api/mandate-activity-provenance">>).
* ''A package names no vessel.'' Every entity starts at the herm floor; a seated persona lights the Lararium stack over it. Only a lararium lights a Pronaos, and a herm mirrors a house's published bytes by CID (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/pronaos#/the-placement">>).
* ''Deferred by the house'': realm feeding (two carriers disagree on max over per-writer slots against a blank merge; it waits for the first-realm arc), community exit, and funding, where payment may buy capacity and never standing.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-road>>

!! The road ~ what stays unbuilt, in the order a builder walks it

''The organizing law'': identity bytes (the Nexus AID, the device edge, the dyad scope, the seats, the
sentinel) change ONCE, inside one train. ''The owner law'': each slice lands its logic in modules, and one
named owner per step commits the one compose hunk in `open-node-vessel.ts` and `open-browser-vessel.ts`;
every other lane stops at a module boundary. Each lane's first commit carries its canon. ''The fence''
(#/rulings) bounds the whole road: every slice that changes a shape lands before the second operator seats a key in the first Nexus.

''The order'': GROUND → SUBSTRATE → HARBOR → KAHU PLANE → ONE FACE → TWO HEARTHS. The re-found's train
rides inside it — R-ENGINE's fold and R-CUSTODY's seal in SUBSTRATE; R-CHARTER → R-WIRE, then R-KAHU,
R-ENGINE's offers and R-EMBER, in the KAHU PLANE. Where the train's older order (the engine after the wire)
and the six paths disagree, operator-ruled order governs: the engine fold and the custody
seal land early, and the container's tail lands before the charter train.

!!! 1 · GROUND ~ the residue (no identity bytes; waits on nothing; rides beside SUBSTRATE on disjoint files)
* ''The receipt ledger's direct leg'': the three receipts of #/rulings stand where a CLI meets its vessel (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/receipt-ledger">>). The leg across a herm rides THE WIRE (`W12` of #/the-loops).
* ''`KeyhiveIdentitySlot` production wiring.''
* ''Retired bags, the evergreen sweep and custody's edges'', itemized in #/the-loops (`G5` through `G7`).
* ''Measures'': the full-corpus per-verb flush re-measure; pack-PIN recursion; a production `assertBodyIndexTier` caller.
* ''Canon direction the build owes'': canon crosses hearth → leaf; a leaf never writes the hearth's canon documents, and edits land in the working layer. The admitted leaf-side mount flag stops mount-path writes, while Repo sync carries those documents in both directions.
* ''A binding lands after its document rests'': a slot mint records the binding only on the vessel store's acknowledgment (the rest door, `doc:rest` / `doc:rested`); a mint no binding names stays listed (`lares vessel unbound`), and nothing removes one.

!!! 2 · SUBSTRATE ~ two SOLO relays, the fold first, then the seal
Each runs one spirit per commit, and every commit leaves HEAD building. No other node lane runs beside the
seal: it owns the compose hunk.

''R-ENGINE, the fold.''
* The R-ENGINE fold target dependency line, in fixed order: automerge-repo and its four listed adapters at alpha.5 → `@automerge/automerge` 3.4.1 → `@keyhive/keyhive` 0.3.0-alpha.1; no Subduction or Onomancy dependency → the genesis re-bake (`GENESIS_ENGINE_CID`, every fixture holding the hearth true-name, the untracked CAS in the same commit) → the fold's own live proof on the rebuilt image, with a pushed shard run reading green. The manifests hold all three dependency steps (`packages/lararium-node/package.json`, `packages/lararium-keyhive/package.json`); a Keyhive Archive and a cap event carry no format tag, so bytes one Keyhive version wrote refuse under another and a version move re-founds every vessel. The fold's own live proof on the rebuilt image stands owed.
* TW5 core and the memetic-wikitext bootstrap fold into `engineCid`; the vocabulary leaves the engine as content; the coal-only seed carries no `schemaVersion`; the island `schema_version` drops its counter.

''R-CUSTODY, the seal'' (P3-SEAL).
* ''What stands sealed'': the custody root and the `identity-anchors` family: the keyhive archive, each face's own veil archive (`veil-archive-h<N>.bin`), each face's anchors and the anchor roster. A founding seals them from its first write and hands out every keyhive Archive it stood before the provider leaves; the veil derives from the sealed custody root; a seat (`custody-seat.ts`) carries one holder per identity home, and each row reads whole or refuses by name, never empty. A creator that stands again restores from its Archive: cap events carry membership and never a creator's key agreement state. A stand mints one contact card and its Archive leaves after that mint, so the Archive lineage opens every prekey the identity ever presented. A browser vessel keeps the Archives its founding and its admit hand out in its keystore beside the device seed, in the clear, until browser custody wraps both (`browser-keyhive-archive.ts`).
* ''Every writer family rests under its custody key'': the carrier table (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/custody#/the-table">>; `vault-carriers.ts`) names five classes: `floor` and `floor-plain` rest outside the vessel's keys under their stated law; `hot` and `cold` rest sealed under the vessel key; `serving` names the rows SK seals: SK mints at the founding, a human unlock opens it through the vessel key's entry in its serving tree (`sk-slots`, a bare `floor` row; `serving-key.ts`, `custody-session.ts`), and the serving rows, the ember slots and an outbox key the vessel key wraps stay owed in code (`E2`, `K13`). Each sealed row passes through the one sealed writer (sidecar → re-open → compare → rename, never sealing in place) and the sealed open. The per-face backup bundle stands built, cut and restore.
* ''No keyring stands'' (operator ruling, #/rulings): the code holds no per-Nexus convergence secret. No custody row, seat mint, admission delivery or charter commitment carries one, and a persona admission's signed transcript names no keyring digest. A private body seals under its bag's content key, which reaches its readers through a lockbox (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/sealed-store#/keys">>). A seat's act and a roll mint nothing beside the charter epoch. The same ruling's standing law binds every later slice: let a counter, wherever one appears, order one writer's own emissions only; keep the face at the session capability; keep every verified fork head visible; retain by causal evidence.
* ''The handle-book'': the recogniser's handle-book rests sealed under the VK. Nothing at its path reads as a book that recognises nobody; a row that stands and does not open, or opens to another shape, refuses by name at every read and every write, and a save lands only over nothing or over a snapshot that reads whole (`packages/lararium-node/src/node-circle-store.ts`). The follow graph holds one home, the persona-scoped circles plane the daemon's circle verbs write, and no custody row carries an edge.
* ''The admit family'': the admissions view, the pending enrollments and the pending grant memos rest sealed under the VK, each read whole or refused by name, each changed under one custody holder. The admission commits a hop: an open binds its offer to the first grant that verifies, signs the acknowledgment, records the admission, then drops the enrollment; an accept verifies against a memo it leaves standing, records, then drops the memo. An act that dies before the record leaves the pending entry, a drop that fails after the record still hands back the acknowledgment, and an offer completes no grant but the one it bound (`packages/lararium-node/src/node-persona-admit-store.ts`, `persona-admit-flow.ts`).
* ''The vessel-identity family'': the persona roster, the worn-face selector, the pet-name map, the declaration map and the published-face record rest sealed under the VK as hot rows; each persona root rests as a cold row, opened for one act; the floor vessel key rests beside its signed inception, every load proves that inception, and no next-key seed or commitment rides it. The roster commits a root: a mint writes the root row, then the roster line that carries the root's public key, so a reading of a public key answers off the hot roster and opens no root. A root row with no roster line reads as an orphan: no reader holds it, `lares persona list` names it, and the same mint run again adopts it. A roster line whose root row went missing, or names another key, refuses by name at the act that needs the seed, and nothing mints over it. A hot row with nothing at its path reads as holding nothing; a row that stands and does not open, or opens to another shape, refuses by name at every read and every write. A vessel key mints only inside a held custody, and lands with its inception under the custody's one claim: the key file commits the mint, so two acts never commit two keys, and a boot loads a key and never mints one. The floor reads no row of the family, so a carried-Nexus reading by persona (a seated chair, a kept consent) answers empty until the key arrives, and a sovereign composition reads the roster whole before it stands (`packages/lararium-node/src/node-vessel-identity.ts`). A floor composition reads no roster, so it shuts every fleet lane: it sorts every device as set aside, sets a proven sibling aside, and its fetch door reads no peer as fleet, the pinned join gate's key included (`packages/lararium-node/src/floor-fleet-lanes.ts`). It still folds the one fleet board its own device edge names in the clear, so the Binding Gate reads this vessel's own seat at every standing.
* ''The device recovery share'': each face's device share rests as one COLD row under the VK, opened for the act that mints it and the quorum that assembles it (`packages/lararium-node/src/recovery-share-store.ts`). Nothing at a face's path reads as none landed; a row that stands and does not open, or opens to another shape, refuses the read and the write by name, so a wrong key replaces no share and no split lands over one it could not read. A share lands once per generation: a second split of the same generation refuses, since it would orphan the off-device shares a person already holds. A split hands the person both off-device shares before the device share rests, so a run that stops between the two leaves nothing sealed and the same command splits afresh.
* ''The walk records'': each hearth a vessel walks at keeps one HOT row sealed under the VK, named by a digest of that hearth's gate key (`packages/lararium-node/src/node-walk-store.ts`). A record holds bearer invites, the hearth's grant and one receipt per carried document (`{ cid: "blake3:<hex>", ref, key }`, whose `key` opens that document), so it counts as a secret row. The row rests in the storage home of the custody seat: the store takes no directory, so no caller files it beside a document store the vessel key never spans. Nothing at a hearth's path reads as no walk there; a row that stands and does not open, opens to another shape, or rests filed under another hearth's name refuses the read, the list and the update by name, and an update seals a whole record or nothing. Each change reads, decides and seals under one custody holder, so one invite leaves a wallet once. A floor composition reads no walk record and presents no walk; an operator's explicit `LAR_JOIN_SYNC` and `LAR_JOIN_GATE` still dial at the floor, with no walk credential, and the first unlock stands a composition that reads the records. A key that locks under a live walk costs the walk one message, which it logs. `lares walk`, `walk take` and `walk invite` ride the custody door (`walk.state` · `walk.take` · `walk.invite`): a carried invite as a verb's argument, or an invite as a verb's outcome, would rest in the daemon's document in the clear.
* ''The hosting rows'': a hearth's hosting state, each spend and its walkers' carriage rest as HOT rows sealed under the VK, in the storage home of the custody seat (`packages/lararium-node/src/hosting-store.ts`, `hosting-carry.ts`). The sealed writer writes a whole file and appends to none, so each spend lands as ONE row of its own, named by a digest of its nonce or marker: the row's presence IS the burn, and it reads, decides and seals under one custody holder. The spend row commits a redemption, and what rides ahead of it re-derives: the grant from the epoch, the leaf, the nonce and the claim, so a crash before the answer replays as `retry`; the operator's counts from the rows standing, by name. A roll's state row commits the roll, and the hosting doc re-derives from it: the next roll and each sovereign stand land an act the sealed state holds and the doc lacks (`landHostingActs`). The sweep of dead epochs' rows passes through the seat's removal door (`removeCustodyRow`), which removes only a row this vessel's key opens; a spend that sealed after a roll retired its epoch burns nothing live and leaves with the next sweep. Each carried value rests as one row whose name carries its address and its length, so a guest's bytes read off its rows and no counter stands beside them. One process serves a hearth's carriage (the standing vessel's own gate, under its store claim), and its contacts and deposits run in turn, so a quota reads the bytes the last deposit left and a reclaim never discards on a reading a contact moved meanwhile. A row that stands and does not open refuses by name: a burn nobody can read never reads as unspent, and a carried value nobody can open never reads as absent. A floor composition reads no hosting row, so a locked hearth sorts no socket as a walker. `lares host`, `host roll` and `host invite` ride the custody door (`host.state` · `host.roll` · `host.invite`): a minted invite as a verb's outcome would rest in the daemon's document in the clear.
* ''The seal ceremony'': the growth rite's two rows rest HOT under the VK in the seal home (`packages/lararium-node/src/seal-ceremony.ts`): `transition-pending.json` prepares a crossing, and `transitions.json` commits its record. A seal verifies the crossing whole, appends its record (the commit point), then removes the pending row, only while that row still holds the crossing it sealed; a seal that committed and stopped resumes to one kept record and no pending row, and kept records with no pending row read clean. A chair's own seat key signs for the ceremony behind the custody door (`seat-key-store.ts`), and only what the standing state licenses: `seal.sign` names a seat by its public key and signs a charter roll that steps from the standing head, reveals at least that head's count of the seats it committed one digest each, seats only keys this vessel holds and re-arms every seat it seats, where the charter's lineage verifies and its head binds the seated roster; it refuses every other image however well formed, and a persona root holds no seat row, so it signs no roll; the rite's hands mark through `crossing.open` · `bind` · `sign` · `witness` · `seal`, which take no image and sign the crossing that stands open (a face's seat keys sign; a witness's root attests). The rite arms itself behind the same door (`seat.arm-genesis`, `seat.arm-roll`): each standing chair's seat key mints there, each chair's NEXT key prints once as a cold-key card the operator reads back before the charter moves (a staged test root alone stands in), a roll adopts each card's key as its chair's seat key, and a superseded seat key retires once no held charter seats it and no open crossing names it (`seat.retire`). An earlier charter shape reads by name (`nexusCharterShape`) and licenses nothing. ''No command file writes a custody row'': a census derives every sealed row's name, every write door and every writer function from the carrier table and the custody modules, and reads red on a command source that calls a door, names a writer function, or names a row beside a file-system write of its own (`packages/lares-cli/tests/custody-write-census.test.ts`).
* ''The face proof's rows'' (specified in code; no production path calls them until the fleet-join door, the removal door and the session open do): `face-proof-key` rests COLD, one face's proof key at one Nexus, held by the root-holding vessel alone, its file named for the face so that face's backup bundle carries it; `vessel-face-key` rests HOT, this vessel's own face-scoped key, and stays on its vessel, outside every bundle. Two doors on the custody seat sign and return (`signAsFace`, `signAsVesselFace`): each signs exactly the image handed in under the key its scope names and hands out no secret, and a store never lands over another standing key (`packages/lararium-node/src/face-key-custody.ts`; the library names the shapes in `FACE_PROOF_CUSTODY_ROWS`).
* ''The gate's two root doors'' (specified in code; no composer calls them until the socket cutover): a floor gate's bundle derives through the floor key's door, which answers at a locked vessel, hands back a fresh child and never the floor key (`vesselFloorKeyDoor`, `packages/lararium-node/src/node-vessel-identity.ts`); a sovereign gate's bundle derives through the custody seat's door, which answers only while the seat holds its key (`custodyGateDoor`, `packages/lararium-node/src/custody-seat.ts`). The doors carry two types, so no line swaps them by accident; a root refuses the other tier's door by name; each seat's door refuses the other tier's child; and a weld holds the two mints to the two seats (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/face-proof#/the-gate-exchange">>).
* ''The gate's memory rows'' (specified in code; no gate calls them until the socket cutover): a sovereign gate's withdrawal memory rests one row per face key in the vessel's storage home, named by the face's proof-subject digest and sealed under the VK, so a locked vessel's disk names no face key (`gate-memory` in the carrier table; `packages/lararium-node/src/gate-memory-rows.ts`). Each write reads, decides and seals under one custody holder, and refuses where it would drop an event the row holds; a locked seat refuses by name. An older copy of a row restored by hand makes a gate forget until the next honest vessel dials, and no fence stands against that hand. A row that stands and does not fold whole refuses by name and reports it (`GateMemoryRowTorn`): the asking gate falls silent, no keep writes over the row, and a person repairs it by hand, where a row read as nothing would let a fresh memory admit a vessel the row once denied. No backup bundle carries the row: a vessel that stands again knows nothing (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/face-proof#/the-withdrawal-proof">>).
* ''The session and the boot'': the daemon stands at the floor holding no VK and unlocks on a custody frame of its owner-only socket (`unlock` · `lock` · `custody` · `act` · `backup`, `packages/lararium-node/src/custody-frames.ts`), handled ahead of every verb, so the credential the CLI reads on its TTY reaches no receipt, outcome or log. One holder keeps the opened VK in node-main (`custody-holder.ts`); an unlock tears the standing composition down and stands a fresh one on the same floor (`open-node-vessel.ts`), a lock returns it to the floor, and three failed unlocks spend a boot's attempts. A composition that refuses above the floor leaves the floor standing and names its fault once. The settle sweeps a sidecar whose write never landed. The test door (`tools/unlock-test-custody.mjs`) opens marked scratch roots alone, and the marker earns one loud line in `vault status` and the boot log. Every boot composes at the floor: nothing stored lifts it. A daemon island holds no VK, so its archive exports cross the custody write door (`custody:write`) and the main thread seals them. Every broad sync port, the daemon island's and each wiki island's, crosses one hardened facade on both ends: an arrival that reads as no sync message drops at the port and never reaches a listener that would throw on it (`packages/lararium-mesh/src/island-repo.ts`). The daemon island's gates ask the main thread what a device's edge reads on its fleet's board through the dyad door (`dyad:ask`), which answers `aside` wherever it cannot read. Each fold of the fleet's edge board sets off the key side's settle on an unlocked vessel, with no timer and no person: the main thread places the island's `seat-duties` verb, awaits its answer and keeps the signed rolls (`packages/lararium-node/src/seat-settle.ts`; <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/kapae#/what-the-code-holds">>). Those two doors and the rest door answer the daemon island's channel alone: each worker's class binds at its spawn (`packages/lararium-node/src/worker-handle.ts`), a wiki island reaches the fetch door alone while its custody write and its rest ask refuse by name and its dyad ask draws `aside`, and a session island reaches no vessel door. A command that acts with no vessel standing opens its own key session at the first sealed row it touches (`packages/lares-cli/src/offline-custody.ts`): a terminal prompt, and under a marked scratch root the published test route. A sealed row's act rides an `act` frame of the custody door and never a store verb: one typed table bounds the acts (`packages/lararium-node/src/sealed-acts.ts`), a standing vessel answers with no second prompt, and beside a standing vessel a command opens no key session of its own, while a root's recovery split refuses there (`packages/lares-cli/src/sealed-act-door.ts`). The fleet mirror of a pet-name and a declared Handle rides that door too: `selves.carry` names a face and which name, the vessel's main thread reads the name off its sealed row and writes it onto that face's own persona plane and no other face's, `lares persona sync` fills and never replaces, and no verb takes or answers a face's own name (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/waking-floor#/the-shape">>).
* ''The per-face backup bundle, its cut'': `lares vault backup --face <N> --out <file>` cuts one face's bundle through the standing vessel's custody door, under the law at <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/custody#/the-cut">>. The carrier table's bundle column picks the rows, the frame asks the vessel's own route again, the daemon island seals the memory section to the pair the bundle key derives, and the command binds the bundle's routes in its own process (`packages/lararium-mesh/src/face-bundle.ts`; `packages/lararium-node/src/face-bundle-cut.ts`, `custody-frames.ts`; `packages/lares-cli/src/vault-backup.ts`). The handle-book tags each record with the face that recognised it, so a bundle carries its own face's records alone. A node vessel's daemon island stands its content-key holder off the document it owns; no content key mints on a production vessel until a session signer stands, so a production cut carries an empty memory section.
* ''The per-face backup bundle, its restore'': `lares vault restore <file>` brings a face back onto a fresh vessel as a recovery act, under the law at <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/custody#/the-restore">>. The vessel checks the root against the newest key history it reads, seals one commit row, lands the carried rows additively, and lights the face through the face rite: a new seat under the same fixed prefix (`packages/lararium-node/src/face-bundle-restore.ts`; the act table's `bundle.restore` and `bundle.import` rows; `packages/lares-cli/src/vault-restore.ts`). The rehearsal runs end to end on scratch roots, on the real doors and on standing vessels of the built tree. `--memory` lands the bundle's content keys through the island's import door; on a production vessel it refuses `signer-unavailable` until the wire cutover opens a session signer.
* ''The custody door and its census'': `lares vault` answers seven verbs (`status` · `unlock` · `lock` · `bind` · `unbind` · `backup` · `restore`), and a credential rides a terminal alone: no flag, environment variable or pipe carries one. `vault status` gives ONE reading of each standing file, derived from the carrier table: a `hot` or `cold` row reads sealed or reads as a fault, a row outside the VK prints the law it rests bare under or reads as a fault where the file breaks that law, and a private file in the three homes that no row names prints by name (`vault-carriers.ts` `outsideTheTable`; `custody-session.ts` `custodyStatus`). `vault unbind --rotate` runs as one act: the rotation's census and its opening of every carrier pass before any byte moves, so a carrier that refuses the rotation leaves the slot bound. `tools/custody-census-witness.sh` founds a vessel on a marked scratch root with the built tree and refuses an owner-only file, a seal or a sidecar outside the table; it carries that rotation to its end on the founded vessel. The law, each bare row's reason and the residue a row's name leaves at rest read at <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/custody#/the-table">>.

!!! 3 · HARBOR ~ the container's tail
* ''A persona door before `up`'': `tools/lares-container.sh` routes CLI acts through the offline door before `up`; after a successful act in a standing vessel the host shim reads the standing verdict and restarts the vessel on `restand`. The verdict reads a LIT face alone, so a `persona wear` that changes the worn face on a standing vessel waits on ONE FACE.
* ''LAN candidate discovery'': the module and wiring stand off by default; `LAR_LAN_DISCOVERY=announce` enables announcements without keys. A real-LAN run and the network mode remain owed (`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/harbor#/lan-discovery`).
* ''Household HTTPS'': the compose `https` profile supplies a terminator and a resolver slot, the `issuer` profile supplies a one-shot DNS-01 issuer that reads the registrar's credential from two owner-read files outside both mounts (`docker-compose.surface.yml`; `tools/household-https-witness.sh` walks issuance against a test authority), the resolver answers each household name with its own address from one records file the operator keeps (`tools/household-resolver-witness.sh` walks it against a test zone), and `deviceAdmitSyncUrl` derives relay reach from `LAR_PUBLIC_URL`. The node entry names itself by that setting alone: a boot without one, or with one that names loopback, says so in one loud line, and the first request that arrives under another host logs once per boot (`packages/lararium-node/src/reach-face-watch.ts`). The operator supplies the domain and the credential files. Owed: the renewal verb with its reload, and a first live issuance. No device takes an install (`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/harbor#/household-https`, `lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/secure-context`).
* ''The harnesses stand on the image'': harnesses run the built image by digest and bind no source tree; the mesh scenarios name their own compose project (`MESH_PROJECT`) and feed their browser vessels from the image; the browser CI runner alone reads the repository read-only. Each harness opens the vessel it staged through the test custody door and reads a hearth as stood only once that door answered an open key; `mesh-scenarios all`, `harbor-walk-witness`, the crossing and herm witnesses, the browser walks and the household HTTPS witness all stand on that shape (`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/harbor#/the-harnesses`).
* ''The release manifest'': `tools/release-manifest.mjs` emits the record shape; a real multi-arch index, its push and signing remain owed.

!!! 4 · KAHU PLANE ~ the realms, the signer, and the one AID train
Build order: the realm and signer shapes against the rulings; then R-CHARTER, the one train that moves
identity bytes; then R-WIRE, so every pin changes once.

* ''Realms'': a vessel stands one plane per realm, and a verb names its realm (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/cabal-realm#/many-realms">>). A cabal reads as its relation closure and takes no key or roster of its own. A cabal realm's documents cross between two operators once the realm's member consult answers for a peer.
* ''The steward signer'': the worn face or a quorum Handle, never the root (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/cabal-realm#/the-steward-signer">>). The seal, the fold and the steward door stand in code; the composing vessel's `realm-bag` verb takes the door through the compose owner's hand. The docker `meme` scenario then proves it by hand at "B edits, promotes", in the asker's order: B asks to keep the bag with her worn face, and A consents.
* ''Thresholded admission and promotion'': the four chartered classes and exact-bytes promotion of #/rulings; chartered Kahu-cabal admission as its own authority act, where a vouch alone admits nobody.

''R-CHARTER, the one AID train.'' Its first stage stands landed (train K of #/the-trains): the chairs sign with seat keys of their own and pre-rotate on cold cards, a Nexus answers to `nexus-<sha>`, the floor key commits no next key, per-Nexus keys mint at random, the PersonaGroup stands as a Keyhive group, and the Stable founding act founds a hearth (`lares vessel found --hearth`): the place, persona zero and a PRIVATE seed charter at one seat, with no keyring, every fleet relation bound to that charter's AID, and every harness that founds a hearth founding through it (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/founding-runbook#/found">>). What stays owed:
* ''The Nexus founding act'': two founders, each from their own vault, seat one key each in a fresh joint genesis at two of two; each reads the other's seat fingerprint aloud; each seat commits its own cold next digest; each founder then signs a carriage contract that both seats countersign (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/kahu#/seed-and-nexus">>). Train N.
* ''The cadence inception'' cited once by the founding act: its shape stands in <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/kahu#/contract">>, and its build waits on who holds a cadence share (#/open; `K9`).
* ''Boards resolve per relation''; one fleet-join door replaces `device-admit` and `persona admit`; add-a-face (A); the bare primary-path seal import goes; the mirror guard keys on posture.

''R-WIRE, the per-Nexus wire key'' (waits on R-CHARTER).
* A per-Nexus wire key in two tiers (operator ruling): a floor route's bundle derives from the floor key and answers at a locked vessel; a sovereign route's bundle derives from the sealed custody root and stands only while the vessel stands unlocked. One gate per (Nexus, route), so knock, pin, verdict and posture read per Nexus exactly (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/face-proof#/the-gate-exchange">>). The serving route's third tier rides train E (`E3`).
* ''Noise IK, proof-first'': the gate emits nothing before a valid first message; the knock becomes the IK pre-message. It retires the `lar:challenge` frame.
* The floor announcement signs under the wire key, folded in here, so gate and announce never sign under two keys.
* One client dial per (hearth, Nexus), so a vessel walks at more than one hearth; until then a second walk hearth refuses aloud.
* The vessel key stops linking a walker's leaves across Nexuses on the wire (`two-maps` clause 5's residual), and the oracle pointer's vessel-key correlation goes with it (`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/pronaos#/the-rung-ladder`).
* ''Per-dial transport keys on the sibling channel'': a leaf proves to a herm under a key fresh per dial, and the leaf-peer transcript binds that key in place of the device key the relay stamps, so a herm colluding with a revoked leaf reads no sibling's device key off its own gate (`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/identity-slot-policy#/the-persona-group-secret`).
* ''Owed (a), the dialer's name rides the clear'': the dialer's ContactCard crosses in cleartext in `lar:auth` over plain `ws://` (`packages/lararium-mesh/src/auth-wire.ts`, `LarAuthMsg`) — a live THR_2 leak that Noise IK's encrypted first message cures, the dialer's static riding inside message one instead.
* ''The admit signature the build owes'': the canon-document list and gate key enter the signed bytes; `device-admit.ts` carries them in `carried`, beside the signed `payload` (`packages/lararium-node/src/commands/device-admit.ts`). The browser's first proof rides `Sec-WebSocket-Protocol` (`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/face-proof`).
* ''Owed (b), the knock tests offline'': the knock path computes an HMAC of the public gate key (`packages/lararium-mesh/src/gate-knock.ts`, `knockSegment`); an observer holding candidate keys tests a sniffed knock offline, so not echoing the key protects only against an observer with no candidates.

''Behind the train, each waiting on R-CHARTER''.
* ''R-KAHU'': S1, a quorum whose seats live on different vessels, and L1, the realm root map from the co-signed `keptBy`. Needs only the seat shape.
* ''R-ENGINE, the offers'': the engine-offer lineage signs under the cadence threshold, adopted within a k-hop window along `prev`.
* ''R-EMBER'': a household's second device lifts a resting hearth to SERVING and to nothing more (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/ember">>). It needs the fleet-join door; the slot tree carries passphrase and paper pins, and the ember pin binds in a serving tree of its own.

!!! 5 · ONE FACE ~ the recorded switch act (waits on the signer and the realm shapes)
* The slice builds `session-contract.mem` in its build order and `face-proof.mem`. A session wears the face; the vessel wears none. The switch acts on ONE session and closes and re-dials only that session's links under the incoming face's scoped proof.
* ''Working-layer law the build owes'': one working document per (face, wiki, slot kind); its address derives from a secret only that face's vessels hold. The working layer follows its wiki across recipe recomposition. A leaf with an admit and no seat writes nothing to face wikis until join completion. Vessel removal leaves the address standing; the gate and document membership refuse the removed vessel (operator ruling).
* A wiki names one face's composition over bags; sharing happens at the bag (operator ruling).
* A personal Handle never rides without its owning face; one current personal Handle per PersonaGroup; a collective quorum Handle stays separate.
* ''Every hand opens a session'' (operator ruling; <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/session-contract#/the-hands">>): a tab, a CLI run, the disk watcher and a spirit's MCP seat differ by capability profile and never by key; MCP and the CLI stand as two doors onto one contract. The slice owes the watcher as a leasing session, the seat under a standing grant, the Mask as a record field, and door parity at the session. A tasked spirit comes down to an AI, a Mask and the vessel's seat, attended or standing; the vessel's own key serves as its system identity and the house mints no other.
* The compose hunk in `open-node-vessel.ts` belongs to this slice's owner; cabal runtime work stops at its module boundary.

!!! 6 · TWO HEARTHS ~ the fresh start, then the ceremony (waits on everything above)
* The fresh start by hand (#/fresh-start) on both machines, on the re-found build.
* The ceremony: both found their seeds through the Stable act and stand; both found the Nexus fresh through the Nexus founding act; each signs a contract the two seats countersign; both join one shared cabal realm beside others; each wears a face; an edit crosses both ways and reads back after a restart on each machine. Probes use synthetic names; no fixture forges a cast persona's key or Handle.

!!! The sealed store ~ five steps threaded through the paths (operator-approved model; step one's format stands as law and in code; each later step lands in its place before the fence, under #/the-attractor's ruling)
''Step three's launch kit'': <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/docs/train-s3-launch">>.
* ''The model'': cleartext lives in the live wiki and in the operator's projected files alone. A private bag rests as authenticated ciphertext chunks, the same bytes at rest and in a blind carrier's hands; a node that holds the capability and stands locked rests sealed. Private bytes take randomized authenticated encryption addressed over the ciphertext, so convergent sealing leaves the private tier. A bag's content key rolls at a causal removal act, never by a clock.
* ''Scope'' (operator ruling): the sealing covers the wiki content stores, node and browser, and the content-addressed store, in carriage as well as at rest. The memory palace, the sensorium, receipts and logs stand outside it. Public and crossroads content stays readable to whoever holds the public keys or lookups.
* ''Faces stand as facets of one operator'' (operator steer): no per-face projection root. Each kind of reader gets named — own vessel, own facet, a friend's face, a hosted guest, a carrier that holds none — before any admission-history rule gets written. One tree on disk serves the person: a projected wiki stands as a surface a session leases, and an edit from disk returns under that session's face (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/session-contract#/the-hands">>). Whoever holds the disk reads every facet's projection and may link them.
* ''Step 1, the one private-byte format'': it stands as law at <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/sealed-store">>, and that carrier names each part the code holds: the sealed value with its ciphertext address and named refusals; the key version, manifest and checkpoint records; the lockbox payload; shareable-history spans; the key lineage and the roll a seat roll feeds. The content-addressed store and the walker's carriage seal and open through it, no opener returns unauthenticated bytes, and no key derives from content or from a Nexus secret. The format changes ONCE, there. The content-key roll stands subscribed to the daemon island's seat rolls. What steps two to five take from the format reads at <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/sealed-store#/owed">>.
* ''Step 2, the key's seat'': a content key's lockbox, the store that rests lockboxes, records and the chunk index, and the content-key holder stand as law at <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/sealed-store#/the-store">>, and that carrier names each part the code holds. A node vessel's daemon island seats the holder beside its Keyhive identities and subscribes the content-key roll to its own seat rolls; a removal's successor wraps for the readers that remain, each lockbox into a Keyhive document of its own, so a removed device opens nothing a vessel seals after it counts the raise. Owed from this step, each to its owner: the session open that hands the island a signer and the chain check, without which no content key mints on a production vessel; a door from the island to the vessel's fold for the raises it counts; the face join's call for a from-now admission or a hand-over; and the browser shore's rest and Archive writer. A raise over the vessel that founded a face's key group names no seat a roll can leave out, so that vessel keeps its keys until `K12` stands a fresh group. What steps three and four take from this step reads at <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/sealed-store#/owed">>.
* ''Step 3, after ONE FACE'': sealed storage values and hydration scoped by the session's face; every fetch, write, catalog lookup and content-addressed read gets checked against that face, since a key alone cannot stop a trusted daemon handing one face another's document.
* ''Step 4, after the KAHU PLANE's seats and wire'': sealed history published to and fetched from a blind carrier by the house's own carriage protocol, with a causal index. A carrier never joins document sync.
* ''Step 5, after ONE FACE'': a public bag served at the floor of a locked node; today the floor serves the oracle snapshot alone.
* ''History at admission'' (operator ruling): sharing a bag's past with a newcomer must have stood declared when those edits got written. An inviter chooses between history the realm already marked shareable and a present-state admission, and never widens the audience for another writer's past. The design target keeps one logical bag name whose key rolls mark epochs: a removal rolls, a from-now admission rolls, and a with-its-past admission hands over the earlier epochs' keys.
* ''Mutual removal'' (operator ruling; <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/kapae">>): both signed acts and both key branches stay, every member sees the conflict, and it ends in an explicit fork unless the realm's charter named a recovery council.

!!! The shore ~ the browser's five rungs, beside the paths
* ''The rungs'': 0, an admitted crossing; 1, a carrier the node holds arrives and renders; 2, a typed field edit reaches the node and survives its restart, read from a fresh browser profile; 3, one human, many faces; 4, two hearths. Rungs 0–2 give the one-machine witness the working-Lararium ruling asks for, and the node's own read decides each rung, never a screenshot or a connected socket.
* ''What blocks rungs 0–2'': rungs 0 and 1 pass live (`tools/browser-ci.sh`, the crossing and render walks); rung 2's leaf half saves and survives a reload of the same profile, while its node half waits on ONE FACE's working layer. The node owes a ''durability receipt'' on the authenticated session (`docUrl`, the stored heads, the holder's boot epoch, sent only after the holder commits those heads; no clock in the frame).
* Device capabilities get measured by `tools/capability-probe/` on real devices, never assumed.
* ''What blocks rung 3 onward'': rung 3 waits on ONE FACE. Rung 4 waits on HARBOR's household HTTPS, on the KAHU PLANE's grant for a foreign leaf, and on browser custody: a browser leaf holds a passphrase or paper slot before it holds a persona root (#/rulings).
* The walks ride `tools/browser-ci.sh`, and build under `lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/dreamdom#/the-ruling`: a frame carries absolute state, and a worker defines no global `window`.

!!! Off this road, by ruling
* ''Cloud carriers'': capability and carrier law, private sealed carriage, per-store lifecycle, causal wake and its measurement. Deferred until the operator can deploy; the reading of "delegated keys" (a narrow carriage grant, or a backup that can act) stays open with them.
* ''The Sensorium'': waits behind the working-Lararium milestone.
* ''Streams'' (`saqimtiaz/streams`, BSD-3-Clause): a deferred crossroads offering; it never rides the genesis boot set.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-loops>>

!! The loops ~ one line per open loop, beside the train that closes it

Each row names what stays owed, the train of #/the-trains that closes it, and who owes a ruling first
(''operator'', ''house'', or nobody). A row names its file so a builder reads the code before leaning on
the row; a line number drifts, so none rests here. Train letters: ''S'' the custody seal · ''G'' ground ·
''K'' the seat · ''N'' the Nexus founding act · ''P'' the Kahu plane · ''W'' the wire and the face proof ·
''E'' the ember · ''O1'' ONE FACE's sessions · ''H'' the hosted road ·
''A'' HARBOR's tail · ''V'' recovery's verbs · ''B'' the browser and the phones · ''X'' TWO HEARTHS.

