* `F11` The hands: the watcher as a leasing session, the seat under a standing grant, the Mask as a record field, door parity at the session (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/session-contract#/the-hands">>). O1.
* `F12` A parallel draft names its writer by the record's own `modifier` value, else by the bare Automerge actor id; neither binds a face, so a reader learns only what a writer chose to say (`parallel-drafts.ts`). The session's face on each change answers it. O1.
* `E1` The ember: the serving tier under its own key, the ember pin, the raise with its count and record, four roads of removal (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/ember">>). Its three questions stand ruled (#/rulings), and `E2` to `E4` carry what each ruling owes. E.
* `E2` The serving key's custody and its unattended road (#/rulings, the ember's key at rest; <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/custody#/the-table">>). Built: the fifth class `serving` in the carrier table, read by the census and `vault status`; SK minted at the founding; `sk-slots` with the vessel key's entry, opened at every human unlock and re-wrapped by a vessel-key rotation. Owed: the serving rows under SK and SK's own rotation; no minting secret in any serving row; the anchor path, where an ember slot answers unattended only against a hardware-sealed hearth key whose attestation the ember checks on each ask; the confirm path, where every slot of a hearth with no such anchor answers only on a person's confirm and threshold two asks a second confirm, never an unattended raise; and the bare deny-only `sk-rotation-owed` mark that any counted removal sets and that refuses every ask until SK rolls (`vault-carriers.ts`, `keyslot.ts`, `custody-seat.ts`). E.
* `E3` The serving gate's tier (#/rulings, the ember's serving gate; <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/face-proof#/the-gate-exchange">>). Owed: a third tier name in the route type, its bundle derived from SK under its own registered domain, its pin, and a pin's move by a signed causal event or a verified key-history step alone, with no quiet period that ages a pin out (`gate-bundle.ts`). E.
* `E4` The raise's commit point and its lapse (#/rulings, how a raise lapses; <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/waking-floor#/the-shape">>). Owed: the count spent at the raise ledger's durable commit, before SERVING composes, so a crash before the commit spends nothing and serves nothing and a crash after it burns the raise; and the raise's lapse at a lock, a restart, a human unlock or a counted removal, with no timer and no heartbeat that ends it (`custody-seat.ts`, the raise ledger's writer). E.

!!! THE HOSTED ROAD
* `H1` The operator's answer and the door that shows what waits (`host door`, `host answer`). H.
* `H2` The hosting act names the operator's seat through one delegation to a hosting key, and carries three dials (`hosting.ts`). H.
* `H3` A walker's token buys a knock unless pre-answered (`socket-sorter.ts`). H.
* `H4` The strangers' dial. H.
* `H5` An invited user's root mints on her device, sealed; the herm carries a sealed copy. B.
* `H6` The hosted phone's admission walk: HTTPS, the on-device mint, a QR code or `lar:` URI, a live leaf. Operator's hands. B.
* `H7` One doctrine line still calls an introduction's lifetime unruled (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/membership-doctrine#/the-invite">>). H, canon.
* `H8` A walker's grant that checks under a public half (#/rulings, the ember's key at rest; <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/ember#/the-tiers">>). The serving tier holds no minting secret, so a serving hearth checks a grant with no key that could mint one. Owed: the grant's signed shape and its public-half check, and the roll that derives the public halves for the serving rows, inside the hosting act's one re-cut and before E's walker arm (`hosting.ts`, `hosting-store.ts`). H.

!!! RECOVERY
* `V2` Guardian enrolment, with the per-face key refusal; the library stands (`recovery-keel-core.ts`). Operator (count and threshold, #/open). V.
* `V3` The memory-card reader. V.
* `V4` The guardian recovery door, the request check, the veto, succession. Operator (#/open). Off this road unless ruled.
* `V5` A restore onto a fresh hearth lands the face beside persona zero, never AS persona zero: the hearth's own founding stands its persona zero first, and the restore's preflight refuses an index where a root already stands, so a persona-zero bundle restores only under `--as <N>` at a free index (`face-bundle-restore.ts`). A person who lost their one machine meets a second face where they expected their own. Lean: a restore road through the founding act itself (`found --hearth` from a bundle), so the restored face stands as persona zero and binds the fresh seed charter. House. V.

!!! THE BROWSER, THE PHONES, TWO HEARTHS
* `B1` Browser custody: the device seed and the Keyhive Archives rest in the clear in the keystore (`browser-keyhive-archive.ts`); a passphrase slot wraps both, a passkey slot joins after its derivation gets measured and never alone, and the clear-password act asks through the confirm door. B.
* `B2` Rung 3: two tabs, two faces, one holder. O1.
* `B3` Device measures owed on the tablet and both phones: owner-kill, the one-tab lock fallback, long background, the passkey derivation, eviction over days (`tools/capability-probe/`). Operator's hands. B.
* `B4` QR pairing: the camera road stands unwired, and no decoder dependency or production decode stands. B.
* `B5` The admit and join path of a browser leaf lacks a real-browser proof; the leaf continuity walk drives the anchor climb alone (`tools/browser-weld/leaf-continuity.mjs`). B.
* `B6` Rung 4: a two-hearth browser walk. X.
* `X1` The automated two-container witness, with no volume in common. X.
* `X2` The ceremony as a walk, in the runbook (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/founding-runbook">>). X.
* `X3` The fresh start by hand on both machines. Operator's hands. X.
* `X4` The parted-authors walk, in the dress rehearsal: two hearths edit one title while apart, rejoin, and each person opens the parallel draft the other left. The walk records whether each recognises their own words and reads why the other version stands live. X.
